    {
      "name": "@page-Plain_Kid",
      "html": "<!-- guest/sections/default.html — what a section with no `data:` resolves to.\n\n     ONE CENTRED SLOT NAMED `body`, at a reading measure.\n\n     THIS IS A FILE AND NOT A BRANCH IN THE RUNTIME, and that is load-bearing\n     rather than tidy. A default that lived in the runtime would be the one\n     section shape nothing else could reach: it would need no HTML file, no\n     `data:` key and no slot declaration, and every other section would be the\n     exception to it. \"The document\" would be privileged all over again —\n     exactly what the deleted render layer kept collapsing into, where one\n     render owned the whole sheet and prose was one column at one measure\n     because there was nowhere else for it to be.\n\n     As a file it is the same mechanism every other section uses. It is read by\n     the server, sent inline in the `page.read` answer like any section's markup,\n     and drawn by the same code path. It is legible, it can be copied as the\n     starting point for a real section, and it can be replaced. A page that is\n     mostly defaults is an ordinary document. The runtime still marks one\n     `data-g-default` so the editor can tell a default section from an authored\n     one; no checker rule reads it.\n\n     THE MEASURE LIVES HERE, ON THE SECTION, RATHER THAN ON THE PAGE. That is\n     what makes a full-bleed section cost nothing: it simply does not carry this\n     file's `max-width`, and there is no sheet-level width for it to fight.\n\n     AND THE MEASURE IS FOR PROSE. When the one slot holds a grid — the\n     document's own table, which the doc plugin makes out of every markdown\n     table it finds in a paragraph and puts in a section of its own with no\n     file of its own, which is this one — the section widens to the figure\n     frame's width and the grid takes it, scrolling sideways inside its own box\n     only past that. The runtime says what a slot holds on the slot, as\n     `data-g-kind`, so this file can size by it without knowing what any\n     plugin draws; a section with a file of its own says its own width. -->\n\n<!-- No colour is set here, deliberately. Every colour in this product resolves\n     to a token the palette rewrites at runtime, and a section that reached for\n     one would be the section that stopped theming. Type and colour are inherited\n     from what the shim declares on `:root` when the theme arrives. -->\n<style>\n  /* `:scope` is this section's own element. The runtime wraps everything in this\n     file in `@scope (#sec-<name>)`, so these rules cannot reach the section\n     above or the section below however ordinary the selectors look. */\n  :scope {\n    display: block;\n  }\n\n  .measure {\n    /* The workspace's own reading measure when its `markdown.yaml` names one,\n       and 34rem when it does not. The scale OFFERS the value on the page root\n       and imposes it nowhere: this file is the section that wants it, which is\n       what keeps a full-bleed section free of it. */\n    max-width: var(--md-measure, 34rem);\n    margin-inline: auto;\n    padding-block: 1.5rem;\n    padding-inline: 1.25rem;\n  }\n\n  /* A GRID IS NOT PROSE. The measure opens to the figure frame's width — the\n     same 64rem the doc plugin's `.wrap` gives a figure — and the slot inside\n     it lets go of the reading measure the frame holds every slot to. An\n     unlayered rule here beats the frame's layered one by existing, which is\n     the frame's own bargain. */\n  .measure:has(> [data-g-kind=\"grid\"]) { max-width: 64rem; }\n  [data-g-kind=\"grid\"] { max-inline-size: none; }\n</style>\n\n<div class=\"measure\">\n  <div data-g-part=\"body\"></div>\n</div>\n",
      "fallback": true,
      "parts": {
        "body": {
          "kind": "child",
          "child": {
            "kind": "page",
            "id": "home/Shadowing/Plain_Kid",
            "name": "Plain Kid",
            "uid": "scopesfixplainkid01",
            "children": false
          },
          "draw": {
            "file": "child.html",
            "html": "<!-- base/child/index.html — HOW THIS PAGE LOOKS INSIDE SOMEBODY ELSE'S.\n\n     This is the one file in `base/` that is not a section. Every page is created\n     with a copy of it in its own directory as `child.html`, and the `child`\n     plugin mounts it wherever a parent draws this page as a child. Delete it and\n     the built-in row comes back, which is a supported act rather than a broken\n     page.\n\n     IT IS MARKUP AND NOT A PROGRAM. A `<script>` in here is removed and\n     reported: a script inserted as markup never runs, so leaving one would be\n     dead code that looks live. The child says how it LOOKS; the section holding\n     it says how the page BEHAVES.\n\n     NO NAME IS WRITTEN INTO IT. `{{name}}`, `{{kind}}`, `{{id}}` and `{{rows}}`\n     are this child's own fields and they are the nearest scope, which is what\n     lets one file sit in every page directory: renaming the page renames the\n     row, where a name copied in here would be wrong the moment somebody did that\n     and nothing on screen would say so.\n\n     NOTHING IN HERE IS FOCUSABLE, ON PURPOSE. The plugin wires the click either\n     way; with no control of its own in the markup it makes the whole drawing the\n     control — role, tab stop, Enter and Space, and an aria-label from the name.\n     Put ONE `<a href>` or `<button type=\"button\">` in here instead if the row\n     wants a real control, and never both: a control inside a thing pretending to\n     be a control is two tab stops for one row.\n\n     Its `<style>` is scoped to the slot by the plugin, so these selectors cannot\n     reach the section above or below.\n\n     IT IS A ROW AND IT STAYS ONE. A name, a count and a kind, and the one thing\n     it does beyond that is LIGHT when the pointer or the keyboard reaches it: a\n     leading edge that is nothing until the row is the one somebody is about to\n     open, in the palette's own hot colour. That is deliberately the whole of the\n     design in here — this file is copied into EVERY page in the workspace, which\n     makes it the most contagious file in `base/`, so what it carries is one\n     touch that says the row is live rather than a look every page inherits.\n     Restyle the copy in a page freely; think twice before restyling this one. -->\n<style>\n  .kid {\n    display: grid;\n    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto auto;\n    align-items: baseline;\n    gap: .55rem;\n    padding: .5rem .65rem;\n    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);\n    /* THE EDGE THAT LIGHTS. Transparent rather than absent, so the row does not\n       move by two pixels the moment the pointer reaches it — the plugin makes\n       the whole drawing the control, so `:hover` and `:focus-within` are already\n       true at the moment they should be and nothing has to be told. */\n    border-inline-start: 2px solid transparent;\n    color: var(--ink);\n    font-family: var(--sheet-face);\n    line-height: 1.4;\n    transition: border-color 140ms ease, background 140ms ease;\n  }\n  .kid:hover,\n  .kid:focus-within {\n    border-inline-start-color: var(--cyan);\n    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--cyan) 7%, transparent);\n  }\n  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .kid { transition: none; } }\n\n  .kid-name { min-inline-size: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }\n\n  /* A COUNT THAT HIDES ITSELF. `{{rows}}` is a table's row count and is EMPTY\n     for a page — the markup cannot branch, so the empty element is what carries\n     the difference, and `:empty` is what reads it.\n\n     THE NUMBER STANDS ALONE, and that is the same fact seen once more. A\n     `::after { content: \" rows\" }` would read \"1 rows\" beside every one-row\n     table, on every page this file is copied into, with no way to spell the\n     other case — the markup cannot branch, so it cannot pluralise either. The\n     `{{kind}}` beside it already says what is being counted. */\n  .kid-count { font-family: var(--gauge-face); font-size: .8rem; color: var(--ink-3); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }\n  .kid-count:empty { display: none; }\n\n  .kid-kind {\n    font-size: .7rem;\n    text-transform: uppercase;\n    color: var(--ink-3);\n    white-space: nowrap;\n  }\n</style>\n\n<div class=\"kid\">\n  <span class=\"kid-name\">{{name}}</span>\n  <span class=\"kid-count\">{{rows}}</span>\n  <span class=\"kid-kind\">{{kind}}</span>\n</div>\n"
          }
        }
      },
      "vars": {
        "who": "the page",
        "rate": 62,
        "unit": "per cent",
        "tags": [
          "north",
          "south"
        ],
        "empty": null
      },
      "source": {
        "name": "@page-Plain_Kid",
        "parts": {
          "body": {
            "type": "child",
            "data": "Plain_Kid"
          }
        }
      }
    }
